WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.603
A week after the move, the family buys a mirror.

2
00:00:05.603 --> 00:00:08.870
It gets a box and a sticky note, like everything else.

3
00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:15.221
And the master list on the fridge, the one sheet that names every box in the house, should gain one line.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.460
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:24.181
Each generated file tells KSP where it came from, in one argument called Dependencies.

6
00:00:24.181 --> 00:00:25.844
What if I get that wrong?

7
00:00:25.844 --> 00:00:27.907
Surely the build would tell me?

8
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.130
First, how KSP saves work.

9
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.988
After an edit, it doesn't show the helper every box again.

10
00:00:33.988 --> 00:00:38.179
It shows him only the dirty ones: the files it must read again.

11
00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:40.907
Every other card comes back from storage.

12
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:47.307
To pick the dirty files, KSP reads a note on the back of every card: made from these boxes.

13
00:00:47.307 --> 00:00:50.308
That note is what you wrote in Dependencies.

14
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.712
Lamp's card says: made from the lamp box, and nothing else.

15
00:00:54.712 --> 00:00:56.497
KSP calls that isolating.

16
00:00:56.497 --> 00:00:59.352
Repack the lamp, and its card is redone.

17
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:07.694
The list on the fridge says more: made from every box on me, and redo me after any change at all, even a brand new box.

18
00:01:07.694 --> 00:01:09.002
That's aggregating.

19
00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:11.275
In code, aggregating equals true.

20
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.704
In the last section, we added a rug that way.

21
00:01:14.704 --> 00:01:19.331
KSP read all five boxes again, and the list grew to five names.

22
00:01:19.331 --> 00:01:22.855
Five dirty files out of seven, about 71 percent.

23
00:01:22.855 --> 00:01:24.397
Not cheap, but right.

24
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.516
Now let's break it on purpose.

25
00:01:26.516 --> 00:01:31.555
The lab's processor has a teaching switch, an option no real processor has.

26
00:01:31.555 --> 00:01:33.100
We set it to isolating.

27
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:38.543
The list's note still names all five boxes, but the part about new boxes is gone.

28
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.028
To be fair, that's not a silly mistake.

29
00:01:41.028 --> 00:01:45.625
Edit the lamp, and the list is still redone, because the note names the lamp.

30
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:51.267
Now add the mirror: a new file, Mirror.kt, with one more @Inventory class.

31
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:54.001
Which boxes will KSP show the helper?

32
00:01:54.001 --> 00:01:56.143
And what will the fridge say?

33
00:01:56.143 --> 00:01:57.325
Pause and guess.

34
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.884
KSP read one file.

35
00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:05.114
Mirror.kt, one out of eight, 12.5 percent.

36
00:02:05.114 --> 00:02:11.805
The mirror is new, so no card's note mentions it, and nothing marked the list as dirty.

37
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.731
So the helper sees one box.

38
00:02:13.731 --> 00:02:20.973
He writes the list of every box he can see, a list of one, and it goes up on the fridge, right over the old list.

39
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.486
Here the picture breaks, and that's exactly the bug.

40
00:02:24.486 --> 00:02:26.635
A family would just add one line.

41
00:02:26.635 --> 00:02:28.132
KSP never edits a list.

42
00:02:28.132 --> 00:02:32.625
The processor writes the whole list again, from the boxes it's shown.

43
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.034
And the build?

44
00:02:34.034 --> 00:02:34.563
Green.

45
00:02:34.563 --> 00:02:36.413
No error, no warning.

46
00:02:36.413 --> 00:02:38.352
Then the program runs.

47
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.795
It prints MovingDayList: Mirror.

48
00:02:40.795 --> 00:02:48.540
Then it asks the list for the box's card, and gets IllegalStateException: MovingDayList has no card for Box.

49
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.662
A move with one mirror in it.

50
00:02:50.662 --> 00:02:53.435
And notice, the helper did nothing wrong.

51
00:02:53.435 --> 00:02:55.667
He listed every box he was shown.

52
00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.711
Our note never told KSP to show him the rest.

53
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.091
The second wrong note is worse.

54
00:03:01.091 --> 00:03:03.917
Dependencies false, with no file at all.

55
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:05.189
Made from nothing.

56
00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:07.097
The list belongs to no box.

57
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:14.128
We switched to none, built once, then added a comment to MovingDay.kt, a file with no sticky note.

58
00:03:14.128 --> 00:03:15.896
Only that file was dirty.

59
00:03:15.896 --> 00:03:22.047
The helper found no box to list, and KSP threw the old list out with the packing paper.

60
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:29.069
This time the compiler does complain: Unresolved reference MovingDayList, in MovingDay.kt, line 14.

61
00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:33.648
It points at the reader of the list, not at the note that lost it.

62
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.777
Honestly, Dependencies with no file should have been called: please delete me later.

63
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.891
The third note plays it safe: Dependencies.ALL_FILES, made from everything.

64
00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:48.533
One comment in Lamp.kt, and every file is dirty.

65
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:53.541
KSP's own docs say it essentially disables incremental processing.

66
00:03:53.541 --> 00:03:54.907
Correct, and slow.

67
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.241
Here's what makes these bugs sneaky.

68
00:03:58.241 --> 00:04:03.057
A clean build fixes all three, because it reads every box from scratch.

69
00:04:03.057 --> 00:04:07.126
So do the kctfork tests, which start clean on every compile.

70
00:04:07.126 --> 00:04:11.805
Only an incremental build, after an ordinary edit, shows the mistake.

71
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.206
Java has one safety net here.

72
00:04:14.206 --> 00:04:22.195
When an isolating javac processor names no source element, Gradle recompiles every file: slow, but right.

73
00:04:22.195 --> 00:04:23.945
KSP believes your note.

74
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.555
So it's tempting to run clean whenever something looks odd.

75
00:04:27.555 --> 00:04:31.303
That's redoing every card in the house because one looked wrong.

76
00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:33.763
It works, and it's how weekends disappear.

77
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:35.888
So here's the rule.

78
00:04:35.888 --> 00:04:42.870
A generated file that's right after a clean build, and wrong after an edit, has the wrong Dependencies.

79
00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.481
Fix them.

80
00:04:43.481 --> 00:04:45.379
Don't make cleaning a habit.

81
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.246
Here's the right note in code.

82
00:04:48.246 --> 00:04:55.639
Read it aloud: this list came from the file of every class on it, each file once, and new files can change it too.

83
00:04:55.639 --> 00:05:00.763
Java names every class as an origin, and marks the whole processor aggregating.

84
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.725
So, does a wrong Dependencies fail the build?

85
00:05:04.725 --> 00:05:04.934
No.

86
00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:08.198
It fails a later build, after an ordinary edit.

87
00:05:08.198 --> 00:05:10.490
A card for one box names its box.

88
00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:14.796
A list of many boxes names them all, and says yes to new ones.

89
00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:15.723
Quick check.

90
00:05:15.723 --> 00:05:22.304
Your processor writes one builder per class, each made from its own class's file, and isolating.

91
00:05:22.304 --> 00:05:23.675
You add a new class.

92
00:05:23.675 --> 00:05:25.252
Does anything go stale?

93
00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:28.614
No.

94
00:05:28.614 --> 00:05:34.808
The new file is dirty itself, so it gets its own builder, and no other output needs it.

95
00:05:34.808 --> 00:05:38.937
Only an output that lists many boxes must say aggregating.
